# Project data — sample CSVs, cached API responses, live captures.
# By default nothing under data/ ships with the repo; users bring their own
# input files and any cached responses they want to reuse.
#
# ...with one deliberate exception: the two parcel use-case notebooks ship
# their inputs AND their cached API responses, so they run end-to-end on a
# fresh clone with no API key and no credits spent. A parcel boundary is not
# something a user can invent the way they can invent a point list, and these
# two notebooks are the ones we demo. ~6.9 MB, mostly base64 imagery in the
# satellite / street-view responses.
#
# Note the shape: git will not descend into an excluded *directory*, so each
# level has to be re-excluded with `dir/*` before a file inside it can be
# re-included. `data/` on its own would make every negation below a no-op.
data/*
!data/parcel_diridon_san_jose_sample.geojson
!data/parcel_portfolio_san_jose_sample.geojson

!data/heatmaps/
data/heatmaps/*
!data/heatmaps/heatmap_parcel_*.json

!data/satellite/
data/satellite/*
!data/satellite/satellite_parcel_*.json

!data/street_view/
data/street_view/*
!data/street_view/streetview_parcel_*.json

!data/env_params/
data/env_params/*
!data/env_params/env_params_parcel_*.json

# HeatCanyon build artifacts
.venv/
# The solved fields. `web/data/*.bin` alone was not enough once the year arrived:
# a gitignore pattern containing a slash is anchored, so it matched the top-level
# binaries and NOT web/data/month_07/thermal.bin — twelve monthly periods and the
# annual planes, about 130 MB, would have gone straight into the repository.
web/data/*.bin
web/data/**/*.bin

# web/data/vo/ is NOT ignored, and that is the point. Those are the opening
# film's narration — one MP3 per line, read by ElevenLabs, about three and a half
# megabytes. Committing them is the same bargain as the FortyGuard responses
# above: they cost money to make, they are deterministic given the script, and
# shipping them means a fresh clone plays the film with the real voice, offline,
# having spent nothing. Take them out and every clone either buys them again or
# gets the browser's speech synthesiser.
#
# `web/data/vo/lines.json` is the index — key to the sentence it is a recording
# of — which is what makes a directory of hashes something a person can audit and
# prune. See docs/HEATCANYON.md, "The voice".

# HeatCanyon caches ARE shipped, deliberately: the FortyGuard responses cost
# credits and the NYC Open Data pulls take minutes, so committing them makes the
# whole pipeline reproducible offline at zero cost and zero API calls.
!data/manhattan/
data/manhattan/*
!data/manhattan/*.json
!data/nyc/
data/nyc/*
!data/nyc/*.json
!data/nyc/*.geojson
tests/screenshots/
test-results/
playwright-report/
node_modules/

# Cached Natural Earth / GISTEMP downloads for the opening film's globe.
# The built assets under web/data/ are committed; these raw sources are not.
data/globe/*
!data/globe/.gitkeep

# The analyst's workspaces and durable run transcripts. One directory per run
# under .agent/runs/<run_id>/ holding frames.jsonl, status.json, and whatever
# scripts and charts the agent wrote. Useful locally, not repository content.
.agent/

# …except the one recorded turn the opening walkthrough plays back.
#
# Chapter five of the film shows the analyst working rather than describing it,
# and what it shows is a real turn — fifty-six tool calls, six re-solves of the
# canyon physics — replayed from its own JSONL by the server. Asking live on
# camera would cost minutes of waiting and money on every play, and would put
# whatever the model said that afternoon in front of whoever is watching.
#
# It is checked in because the film depends on it and a run directory is not
# reproducible: clear it and chapter five plays with an empty console.
#
# Re-including one file out of an ignored tree takes five lines, and the order
# matters. Git will not look inside an excluded directory, so each level has to
# be re-admitted before the level below it can be excluded again — hence the
# alternation: open `.agent/runs/`, shut every run in it, open this one, shut
# everything in this one, then name the two files the replay actually reads. The
# workspace of scratch scripts the turn wrote stays out.
!.agent/
!.agent/runs/
.agent/runs/*
!.agent/runs/r17880442193644fa/
.agent/runs/r17880442193644fa/*
!.agent/runs/r17880442193644fa/frames.jsonl
!.agent/runs/r17880442193644fa/status.json

# The design brief this overhaul was worked from — a 10 MB zip of reference
# imagery that was an INPUT to the work, not part of it. The result is in
# web/css/ and web/js/; the brief does not need to ship with it.
Design Overhaul Request.zip
*:Zone.Identifier

# The tree scripts/deploy_hf.py assembles for the Space: the repository's source
# plus the gitignored solved fields, minus everything the pipeline needs and the
# server does not. Rebuilt from scratch on every deploy, so it is never a source.
.deploy/
